<div class="section"><h2>Session Health — What Every State Means</h2><div class="note">Lifecycle tells what happened to the HttpSession. Live Health tells whether the session is usable in the current Tomcat runtime. PostgreSQL keeps the history after the session dies.</div><div class="states">
<div class="state green"><h3>● ACTIVE + RECENT = HEALTHY</h3><p>The HttpSession exists in this Tomcat runtime and recent HTTP activity proves the client is using it.</p><ul><li>JSESSIONID is currently valid.</li><li>Tomcat owns the session in memory.</li><li>Recent request evidence is under two minutes old.</li></ul></div>
<div class="state amber"><h3>● ACTIVE + NO RECENT REQUESTS = STALE</h3><p>Tomcat still considers the session valid, but the client has not made a recent request.</p><ul><li>User may simply be idle.</li><li>Not automatically a failure.</li><li>It can become active again before timeout/destruction.</li></ul></div>
<div class="state blue"><h3>● DESTROYED = ENDED NORMALLY</h3><p>Tomcat intentionally removed the session.</p><ul><li>Logout, timeout, invalidation, or normal destruction.</li><li>No longer live in Tomcat memory.</li><li>History remains for troubleshooting.</li></ul></div>
<div class="state purple"><h3>● ABANDONED_ON_RESTART = RESTART TERMINATED</h3><p>Tomcat/application restarted while the session was active.</p><ul><li>Old JVM/in-memory session disappeared.</li><li>Live Health must be NOT ACTIVE.</li><li>A later browser request creates a new session.</li></ul></div>
</div></div>

<div class="section"><h2>Application Execution Flow — From Tomcat Start to Response</h2><div class="note">One logical session can use many worker threads and many database backend processes across its lifetime.</div><div class="flow">
<div class="step"><div class="num">1</div><b>TOMCAT / JVM START</b><span>JVM starts, WAR deploys, connector opens, listeners initialize. Orphaned ACTIVE rows are marked ABANDONED_ON_RESTART.</span></div>
<div class="step"><div class="num">2</div><b>HTTP ARRIVES</b><span>Tomcat accepts the request and assigns an available http-nio worker.</span></div>
<div class="step"><div class="num">3</div><b>SESSION RESOLUTION</b><span>Tomcat reads JSESSIONID. Existing session is reused or a new HttpSession is created.</span></div>
<div class="step"><div class="num">4</div><b>SERVLET / JVM THREAD</b><span>Servlet executes on the assigned worker. Thread name and JVM TID are captured.</span></div>
<div class="step"><div class="num">5</div><b>DEPENDENCIES</b><span>Application can borrow JDBC, query PostgreSQL, use Redis, publish RabbitMQ, or call Stripe.</span></div>
<div class="step"><div class="num">6</div><b>TELEMETRY</b><span>Request ID, session, URI, status, latency, worker, JVM TID and PostgreSQL PID are persisted.</span></div>
<div class="step"><div class="num">7</div><b>RESPONSE / REUSE</b><span>Response returns. Worker goes back to the pool. Session remains, idles, ends, or is lost on restart.</span></div>
</div><div class="help"><b>Thread inspection:</b> click a worker/TID below. You will get that one thread's live Java stack, state, CPU time and locks. This avoids a full thread dump. Because Tomcat reuses workers, a live stack clicked later is not automatically the historical stack that existed during an old request.</div></div>
